#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087601 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "8ba9598f-a266-57cb-8ef5-9757dd612a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-6607" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} -4 \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} +4 y = 8x + 4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root $\\lambda =  2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =8x+4 \\\\ 4\\alpha x+(4\\beta -4\\alpha ) & =8x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\beta -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=2, \\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "1b78a582-0bfa-53dc-b392-99b7da223ff6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6608" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "83788240-c717-5e86-8753-ca320f4c979a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6721" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} - \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y = 6.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = -1, 2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ {- x} + B e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0,$ which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0,$ or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-0-2\\alpha =6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = -3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "25b04b8f-7447-572f-8600-9d11e5423c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6786" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x} - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3,\n\\qquad y(0) =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\ \\ y'(0) = 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B -\\dfrac{1}{2}  = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nA-2B -3 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ A-2B=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x}  - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14828a0b-2213-569f-86f1-8e31151be09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432504} {:answer/id #uuid "34c64c79-64ac-5c23-b537-626f39845954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432510} {:answer/id #uuid "386fd9b0-705c-542d-b41b-c9a558f41c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432511} {:answer/id #uuid "85c0c468-85db-5d60-8852-1cee6d6782f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432523} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e3f7ee-a71c-5552-9776-afb6cd37b76e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=4x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14828a0b-2213-569f-86f1-8e31151be09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432504} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d1e661-86cd-5c5e-bccb-65d2d5c5f0e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a601b5b-b013-5c42-bdd5-a25b3a11ff4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64284" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=-16\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-8\\alpha =-16\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5720f24-4834-5f92-98ef-62d15aa34022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{x}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403126} {:answer/id #uuid "94298549-9f11-59e3-9778-348dfb75c6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403127} {:answer/id #uuid "a894006f-0a61-540c-bca8-073ac57ffb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403128} {:answer/id #uuid "98f1d505-2a8f-586d-b7f5-1495b711f3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403129} {:answer/id #uuid "9f49a9b7-19a3-54bd-a9db-ec8551633352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98f1d505-2a8f-586d-b7f5-1495b711f3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403129} :answer-field/id #uuid "090c78d3-3a0c-5fa4-b1fc-517e968e51e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d511e4f-8803-5021-b5c2-53a7b4de5acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64285" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=-5x+6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha -5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x+(-4\\alpha -5\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a85e00-002e-526f-aa68-9fa9a9e3cd49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342517} {:answer/id #uuid "3f517e31-a1d3-5caa-8710-7ee592d9e883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342760} {:answer/id #uuid "565ce293-b429-5b97-aecf-cf9c7e935987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342957} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f3d97f-80c2-5792-afc6-911099c236aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343698} {:answer/id #uuid "e06fa21a-8c01-5a09-8cc4-778680199c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+3x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5f3d97f-80c2-5792-afc6-911099c236aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343698} :answer-field/id #uuid "84373811-cac1-5c50-acaa-26cce938f12f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a92cf087-3d29-5414-a809-a999e198051b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77763" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -3(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\beta -6\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\beta -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =6,\\,\\gamma =6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53031c9e-3029-5c89-b063-5ccb942ef9c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346059} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2eaf7f-fcdb-5fea-8828-d7c11a09e69b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346567} {:answer/id #uuid "b71804c0-e438-5968-a64c-04648858fbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346947} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ec8f13-952d-5419-9dbe-a75028b116ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347113} {:answer/id #uuid "d8decbe4-3b1e-515c-be19-386620836e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b71804c0-e438-5968-a64c-04648858fbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346947} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec4db00-a997-5e7a-a546-2f8ffac2ce10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "870e7b8e-206b-50c2-8d89-7f75a9431965" :question/math-academy-id "q-7901" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{4}{3},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=0,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{4}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, $A=1.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(B-3A){e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nB-3A=5.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=8.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51c26de1-64db-53ff-a3b5-4cb2aa7cbbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346055} {:answer/id #uuid "834b955e-0e91-5d48-93b7-078f0a7d9b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346494} {:answer/id #uuid "b6361fcf-d8fb-5366-b2bd-4a6d76859ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346938} {:answer/id #uuid "c131fa8e-a30c-5300-a468-a2856e033e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347021} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a12b0d-40e2-5ccc-9c14-7c223f437b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51c26de1-64db-53ff-a3b5-4cb2aa7cbbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346055} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c068d9-5cbe-5ce9-a70e-f7047cf5378b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "513cfc62-eb9a-585a-a155-a58861456868" :question/math-academy-id "q-7902" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=32x+16.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha +16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n16\\alpha x+(8\\alpha +16\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha =32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 8\\alpha +16\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e011e8-4a7a-508d-bca5-385ded875ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345360} {:answer/id #uuid "03917c72-354d-53bd-8eb1-b175d01190d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345383} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6f8c3a-e267-5331-9844-155baa68208c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345469} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba85172-106c-541a-a67c-8d14ff82d144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-5x}+{x}^{2}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345590} {:answer/id #uuid "7677da2d-0340-547c-ba24-118238fab432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00e011e8-4a7a-508d-bca5-385ded875ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345360} :answer-field/id #uuid "92191c63-14bc-50e1-b8ca-0152f65d00c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "445996cc-ad9d-514f-8bb7-d053c9192103" :question/math-academy-id "q-7903" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=25{x}^{2}+5x-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -10(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n25\\alpha {x}^{2}+(25\\beta -20\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}25\\alpha =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 25\\beta -20\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12dc8059-997f-5b70-b19a-53e42291827a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432503} {:answer/id #uuid "412bacb2-eb4e-5263-b503-28189ebf9dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432513} {:answer/id #uuid "7685bde3-7f9c-5aad-beaf-416e828c0c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=5x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432519} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6feca8-28da-52be-aa45-699792065d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432526} {:answer/id #uuid "b06ff9dc-666a-5b56-8877-35bccf5bd287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412bacb2-eb4e-5263-b503-28189ebf9dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432513} :answer-field/id #uuid "c30934d7-3df8-5273-82c7-2ca133d40516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04161b97-6524-5604-a81b-7440c052538a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79615" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=-15\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+3\\alpha =-15\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b61525-d20a-548a-b756-9e59c5c5df90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371100} {:answer/id #uuid "d804cefe-dc65-5653-bac7-1ee49d119f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371101} {:answer/id #uuid "dd45f29b-ae24-5c96-b86f-5216971fb4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371102} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3bac32-61c1-54c0-8921-9bc005fa5675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371103} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6182a3-ea22-5ea5-8440-04b1c46b656c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe6182a3-ea22-5ea5-8440-04b1c46b656c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371104} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd83c91-8d54-5d91-bd2e-603f01529164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9cf2e1e-2a40-5120-879a-34cfa64cf58d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79616" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=3{x}^{2}+2x+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+3(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=3{x}^{2}+2x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n3\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +3\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +3\\gamma )=3{x}^{2}+2x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +3\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +3\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-2x+3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15778996-cdd8-5831-ad7c-e67b73d56232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x}-3{e}^{3x}+3x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432505} {:answer/id #uuid "7effc4af-f878-5527-b914-5befa2220dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{3x}+x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432521} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bdb96e-b91c-5c32-86a3-b964f861d839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432528} {:answer/id #uuid "b067daa9-ff5c-5bc0-aedf-7b8b1adc79e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}+2{e}^{-x}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432532} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9b78b8-543b-5df6-816a-f1a0a50baff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}+3x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15778996-cdd8-5831-ad7c-e67b73d56232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x}-3{e}^{3x}+3x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432505} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ce655f4-7e54-54d7-988a-30dceb709c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcffa98b-601b-574f-8230-ffea834e6b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-79617" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=18x+3,\\,y(0)=3,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x+3.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+3x+3.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=0.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+3B{e}^{3x}+3\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n2A+3B+3=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ 2A+3B+3=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=3,B=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{2x}-3{e}^{3x}+3x+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab841b6-2b4a-5ee2-b66b-fcf171e89172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345991} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc448e1-0a31-5de7-9fe2-10f49c01aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346144} {:answer/id #uuid "c72c0a78-04f7-5822-9ad8-21c54916589a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347082} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e9726e-70b0-54ca-8265-d30ce7498043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347510} {:answer/id #uuid "f9515ac3-2583-53df-adc9-71966a869081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9515ac3-2583-53df-adc9-71966a869081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347554} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c7102b-1630-5782-a186-4a8f63eeea61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddb29a9d-408f-5d53-82cb-356f77e1a234" :question/math-academy-id "q-8034" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=13{x}^{2}+15x-24,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral, ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+26(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n26\\alpha {x}^{2}+(26\\beta +4\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}26\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 26\\beta +4\\alpha =15\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$  \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP-1=2.\n$$\n\nThus, $P=3.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}[(5Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-(5P+Q)\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x]+x+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5Q-P+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=3$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12252c8f-5453-5e4e-b2bf-ee63cc91f153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432502} {:answer/id #uuid "584447c3-a0a8-585a-9550-01ba70c01a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432516} {:answer/id #uuid "a90b5cac-9387-51f8-831b-798a9db0f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432530} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9d5ff6-cdfd-5216-b917-fd9368e1cddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432536} {:answer/id #uuid "fa752cfe-5da5-57d3-bbc4-95d38f70607d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a90b5cac-9387-51f8-831b-798a9db0f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432530} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ce7558-c826-500a-94a1-595a2a54902b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffc3a9b8-ace7-50b5-8eec-e5287c7960c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-93667" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=-12\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+4\\alpha =-12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbd1225-75b1-5aa9-aae5-e64c856e5407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432508} {:answer/id #uuid "71a89a81-c0f6-500f-8508-959c02d4fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432517} {:answer/id #uuid "736c8b92-7564-5851-82c2-bedf047d82fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432518} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bd8cbe-0f64-58a1-8c7a-18b884522ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432527} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ab225a-e4ce-5762-8f58-5846bf9a0aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1ab225a-e4ce-5762-8f58-5846bf9a0aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432534} :answer-field/id #uuid "14054356-ff2f-545f-8033-5c1431d3a511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62a4e5ba-fa3d-5d0d-9956-72e1733faeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-93671" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=-12.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-12\\alpha =-12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "335feeb7-1626-5acb-a58c-0358339a7b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355116} {:answer/id #uuid "6b592210-c753-5ff9-9c34-8ef1af145014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355614} {:answer/id #uuid "79848504-e602-53d9-9a9d-226978d6bc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355727} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbc759f-b136-5dd3-b320-f9fe2b45cba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355933} {:answer/id #uuid "ef35e9f6-8ca2-5414-8174-5a35e0078136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef35e9f6-8ca2-5414-8174-5a35e0078136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356750} :answer-field/id #uuid "0603be84-0f83-59e1-81b0-550df6722dca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d86c7083-2294-58a9-8fb2-8327c435d348" :question/math-academy-id "q-93672" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24696ba3-8213-599b-a9db-178115283485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432507} {:answer/id #uuid "81a885a2-4fe8-5053-a20e-381b11e3de3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{x}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432522} {:answer/id #uuid "8b901146-c44a-5649-8fda-95bc77f1d082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432524} {:answer/id #uuid "a82bb5b0-8383-5fb4-beda-6e3a4b3ede52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432529} {:answer/id #uuid "f5051395-088e-5afb-9494-835af8e3a03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{x}{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81a885a2-4fe8-5053-a20e-381b11e3de3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{x}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432522} :answer-field/id #uuid "46792128-fb00-526a-8931-288f98945061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffc50f3b-27ee-5817-a670-1155673ba640" :question/math-academy-id "q-93784" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+20y=18-10x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+4\\alpha +20(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18-10x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n20\\alpha x+(4\\alpha +20\\beta )=18-10x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}20\\alpha =-10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\alpha +20\\beta =18\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "934ee56b-b088-5533-ad4c-e3032ce0c524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404581} {:answer/id #uuid "058adaab-a69e-5815-b046-5007fd02b2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404582} {:answer/id #uuid "a209fd47-aee2-5925-bf14-e92cdd3c3c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404583} {:answer/id #uuid "36905ca1-84ae-504d-9cfb-76b204089ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404584} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2cc0e9-db58-596d-8e97-547728c8fbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d2cc0e9-db58-596d-8e97-547728c8fbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404585} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a0d7ea-2403-5870-baf3-f1e3941ec6cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "311b05cd-8042-5d05-b094-868c3c494826" :question/math-academy-id "q-93785" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3x+4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+2\\alpha +(\\alpha x+\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n\\alpha x+(2\\alpha +\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4ed55d-fca3-5a1a-85aa-44e65f648b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355327} {:answer/id #uuid "55ec1844-c660-5eaf-8c7f-4f07bd6f97d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355431} {:answer/id #uuid "637aa6b7-1ffb-5de6-890e-e7d29913306b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355549} {:answer/id #uuid "846c5112-b915-5771-a0fe-0d1e16e6a962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355831} {:answer/id #uuid "e20a27bd-dac9-557e-90a5-5c14dcaa5eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e20a27bd-dac9-557e-90a5-5c14dcaa5eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356640} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ed96197-af5b-5b12-bb2c-598105826db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "431e20fe-7075-5147-8313-e3b2fa443237" :question/math-academy-id "q-93811" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=18x+3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3,$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(-\\alpha -6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -\\alpha -6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f999a1-ce81-59d8-9b92-dca27584c058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}+x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432512} {:answer/id #uuid "4a00da85-eeca-578d-a926-23d16b3a52cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}+3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432514} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5ec682-f307-5e12-87c3-d24892ceb01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432520} {:answer/id #uuid "a9228497-bb26-5e15-9674-a44c0b7c0c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}-2x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432531} {:answer/id #uuid "e4080e57-38f9-56a0-b937-940d78aaf0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}-3x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a00da85-eeca-578d-a926-23d16b3a52cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}+3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432514} :answer-field/id #uuid "f62cf88f-9f0a-5d6c-9bdd-b3fde8d0cb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4d62141-8603-59c2-87a3-6eb4ab644ea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-93828" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-y=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha {x}^{2}-\\beta x+(2\\alpha -\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ -\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -\\gamma =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =3,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98457a6f-2eb2-5f65-b07b-9d0471a1ae7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371252} {:answer/id #uuid "22f7247c-bbec-57c3-a6cf-ab9990130c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2{x}^{2}-2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371253} {:answer/id #uuid "b929420e-728d-5b68-af34-594441565487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}-2{x}^{2}-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371254} {:answer/id #uuid "4b596bb7-1a0f-570c-9084-cef33b2445cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371255} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b0ac43-f548-5ff0-87db-415578f14416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b596bb7-1a0f-570c-9084-cef33b2445cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371255} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d23e8b-11b4-5ec1-a02e-b04b713d3e20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d5d66cf-660f-56ca-bb31-77faaca8db97" :question/math-academy-id "q-93831" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=5{x}^{2}-4x+5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3\\pm \\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +6(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+10(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n10\\alpha {x}^{2}+(12\\alpha +10\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 12\\alpha +10\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cf28cfe-a5e2-54a1-b1b4-c1f5a0ee1b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-4x}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432506} {:answer/id #uuid "339e38b5-5e8f-5286-b9e3-d16eb0b9da54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+{e}^{-x}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432509} {:answer/id #uuid "5593be39-3fd6-52bf-a2c5-285961b80b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-4x}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432515} {:answer/id #uuid "8ceb6c34-4df1-5080-8a0a-e575486ad5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{-x}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432525} {:answer/id #uuid "f669f7cf-7ee4-549a-a26b-f9aca7aa2566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f669f7cf-7ee4-549a-a26b-f9aca7aa2566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432539} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa5e60eb-ddee-5866-97c5-4793c90dd9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ad0a4da-a37e-5e52-a84f-310fd3db4009" :question/math-academy-id "q-94261" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=4x+1\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-x}+x-1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=4x+1,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-1 & =1 \\\\ A+B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4x}-B{e}^{-x}+1,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4A-B+1 & =-1 \\\\ -4A-B & =-2 \\\\ 4A+B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=2 \\\\ 4A+B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-x}+x-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ace747-f647-5e16-8d49-73c1ef8270dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355322} {:answer/id #uuid "4eda03f4-ad2b-5e8a-8eeb-1bb716e473cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355368} {:answer/id #uuid "d055c881-3067-5a66-b865-90f679d234fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356486} {:answer/id #uuid "dfab3e2c-f0d8-5382-89ec-ba3b9230f2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356621} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0d5f81-1162-5159-88c0-7944f7d48a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d055c881-3067-5a66-b865-90f679d234fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356486} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddaa2fc5-3a2c-5dc7-873c-f7e09062158e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc6aef37-e2a2-539b-a568-364586603b78" :question/math-academy-id "q-94329" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-1 & =0 \\\\ P & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}[(2Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-Q-2P)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]-2x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q-P+1 & =4 \\\\ 2Q-1+1 & =4 \\\\ Q & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1.\n$$"}]}
